WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.820
Welcome to Organic Oxygen.

00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:06.040
This recorded Chemistry class uses an AI generated Marin voice.

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:11.820
An alcohol has hydroxyl attached to a saturated carbon; a phenol has hydroxyl

00:00:11.820 --> 00:00:13.320
directly on an aromatic ring.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:16.580
An ether links two carbon groups through oxygen.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.780
An aldehyde has a carbonyl carbon attached to at least one hydrogen, whereas a

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.500
ketone has two carbon substituents.

00:00:24.420 --> 00:00:28.160
A carboxylic acid contains a carbonyl and hydroxyl at the same carbon.

00:00:29.320 --> 00:00:32.260
Classify alcohols by the number of carbon groups attached to the carbon

00:00:32.260 --> 00:00:36.180
carrying hydroxyl, not by counting every hydroxyl in the molecule.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.560
Ethanol is primary, isopropyl alcohol secondary and tert butyl alcohol

00:00:41.560 --> 00:00:42.000
tertiary.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.300
This classification helps predict oxidation and dehydration, but the reagent

00:00:47.300 --> 00:00:49.560
still decides what reaction actually occurs.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:54.960
Compare ethanol with dimethyl ether: they share a molecular formula but differ

00:00:54.960 --> 00:00:56.620
in connectivity and hydrogen bonding.

00:00:57.380 --> 00:01:01.860
A carbonyl oxygen does not make a compound an alcohol, and two oxygens do not

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:03.720
automatically make a carboxylic acid.

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:06.300
Trace the bonds around the functional carbon.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:10.496
An ester has an oxygen bonded to a carbon group in place of the acid's

00:01:10.496 --> 00:01:10.900
hydroxyl

00:01:10.900 --> 00:01:11.280
hydrogen.

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:15.860
A primary alcohol can oxidize to an aldehyde under suitable controlled

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:20.760
conditions, or further to a carboxylic acid with stronger aqueous oxidation.

00:01:21.580 --> 00:01:23.420
A secondary alcohol gives a ketone.

00:01:24.060 --> 00:01:28.445
A tertiary alcohol lacks the hydrogen on its hydroxyl bearing carbon needed

00:01:28.445 --> 00:01:28.620
for

00:01:28.620 --> 00:01:33.620
the usual direct oxidation; harsh oxidation may instead break carbon carbon

00:01:33.620 --> 00:01:33.880
bonds.

00:01:34.780 --> 00:01:37.960
Acid catalysed dehydration removes water to make an alkene.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:01:41.320
Protonating hydroxyl makes water a better leaving group.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.160
Secondary and tertiary substrates can involve carbocations and rearrangement;

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:50.740
do not require a free primary carbocation for every primary alcohol.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:55.140
Heating ethanol with suitable acid can form ethene, while different

00:01:55.140 --> 00:01:57.900
temperatures and conditions can favour ether formation.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:03.020
For ethanol, controlled oxidation retains both carbons in acetaldehyde.

00:02:03.540 --> 00:02:07.400
Further oxidation gives acetic acid without adding another carbon.

00:02:08.440 --> 00:02:12.680
Reducing acetone instead gives isopropyl alcohol, the reverse functional group

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:14.640
change of secondary alcohol oxidation.

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.758
In dehydration the oxygen leaves as part of water, so oxygen accounting

00:02:19.758 --> 00:02:20.180
differs

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:20.900
from oxidation.

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:25.767
Compare products by both atom inventory and functional group rather than by

00:02:25.767 --> 00:02:25.920
the

00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:26.560
word heating alone.

00:02:26.928 --> 00:02:31.228
Phenol is more acidic than an ordinary alcohol because the phenoxide conjugate

00:02:31.228 --> 00:02:33.728
base benefits from resonance delocalization.

00:02:34.808 --> 00:02:39.248
Carboxylate ions share negative charge over two oxygens and are generally more

00:02:39.248 --> 00:02:40.048
stabilized still.

00:02:40.968 --> 00:02:45.088
These are comparisons under comparable conditions, not permission to apply one

00:02:45.088 --> 00:02:47.528
acidity ranking to every substituted molecule.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:53.288
Phenol reacts with aqueous hydroxide to form phenoxide but ordinarily does not

00:02:53.288 --> 00:02:57.088
release carbon dioxide from bicarbonate as a carboxylic acid does.

00:02:58.088 --> 00:03:01.708
Its hydroxyl group activates the ring and directs electrophilic substitution

00:03:01.708 --> 00:03:03.628
towards ortho and para sites.

00:03:04.648 --> 00:03:09.388
Bromine water can give 2,4,6 tribromophenol; controlled nitration can give

00:03:09.388 --> 00:03:10.468
ortho/para products.

00:03:10.468 --> 00:03:15.728
Reimer Tiemann formylation gives an ortho hydroxy aldehyde as the usual major

00:03:15.728 --> 00:03:17.888
product after work up.

00:03:17.888 --> 00:03:19.408
Substituents can change these comparisons.

00:03:19.808 --> 00:03:23.828
An electron withdrawing group can stabilize phenoxide and increase phenol

00:03:23.828 --> 00:03:26.928
acidity, while its position affects resonance participation.

00:03:28.128 --> 00:03:31.588
The phenolic proton is not the same as a hydrogen attached to ring carbon.

00:03:32.308 --> 00:03:36.108
In Reimer Tiemann chemistry, a new carbon is added to the ring as part of an

00:03:36.108 --> 00:03:37.088
aldehyde group.

00:03:37.808 --> 00:03:42.088
Count seven carbons in the usual salicylaldehyde product compared with six in

00:03:42.088 --> 00:03:42.448
phenol.

00:03:42.648 --> 00:03:47.168
The carbon oxygen double bond is polarized, making carbon electron deficient.

00:03:47.788 --> 00:03:51.948
A nucleophile forms a bond to carbon while the pi electron pair moves towards

00:03:51.948 --> 00:03:55.108
oxygen, producing a tetrahedral intermediate.

00:03:56.088 --> 00:03:58.308
Proton transfer then gives the appropriate product.

00:03:59.208 --> 00:04:02.968
Simple aldehydes are often more reactive than comparable ketones because they

00:04:02.968 --> 00:04:06.368
have less crowding and less electron donation from alkyl groups.

00:04:07.688 --> 00:04:11.348
Cyanide addition followed by protonation gives a cyanohydrin under suitable

00:04:11.348 --> 00:04:11.888
conditions.

00:04:11.888 --> 00:04:17.468
Hydroxylamine can form an oxime, and suitable ammonia derivatives form other

00:04:17.468 --> 00:04:20.268
carbonyl derivatives through addition followed by water loss.

00:04:21.308 --> 00:04:25.168
A Grignard reagent adds a carbon group and, after acidic work up, gives an

00:04:25.168 --> 00:04:25.448
alcohol.

00:04:26.268 --> 00:04:29.088
Acidic protons or moisture can quench it before this addition.

00:04:29.908 --> 00:04:33.428
A trigonal planar carbonyl carbon becomes tetrahedral during addition.

00:04:34.068 --> 00:04:38.128
If the final carbon has four different substituents, a new stereogenic centre

00:04:38.128 --> 00:04:38.668
may result.

00:04:39.148 --> 00:04:44.088
Under ordinary achiral conditions, attack on either face can give enantiomeric

00:04:44.088 --> 00:04:47.148
products rather than one guaranteed pure stereoisomer.

00:04:48.368 --> 00:04:52.028
This geometric consequence is separate from whether an aldehyde reacts faster

00:04:52.028 --> 00:04:52.688
than a ketone.

00:04:53.488 --> 00:04:55.668
Always inspect the product's four attached groups.

00:04:56.448 --> 00:05:01.788
A suitable hydride reduction converts an aldehyde to a primary alcohol and a

00:05:01.788 --> 00:05:03.208
ketone to a secondary alcohol.

00:05:03.928 --> 00:05:05.988
This retains oxygen as hydroxyl.

00:05:05.988 --> 00:05:11.168
Clemmensen reduction under acidic conditions and Wolff Kishner reduction under

00:05:11.168 --> 00:05:15.708
basic conditions instead convert a compatible carbonyl group to methylene.

00:05:16.988 --> 00:05:19.668
Choose conditions that the other functional groups can tolerate.

00:05:20.668 --> 00:05:25.508
Aldehydes are readily oxidized to carboxylic acids or their salts in common

00:05:25.508 --> 00:05:29.608
tests; ketones generally resist these mild oxidations.

00:05:31.028 --> 00:05:35.328
Tollens' silver mirror response supports an oxidizable substrate, but is not

00:05:35.328 --> 00:05:40.368
uniquely diagnostic of every aldehyde: some other reducing compounds respond

00:05:40.368 --> 00:05:40.848
too.

00:05:41.728 --> 00:05:45.828
Ordinary ketones are usually negative, while alpha hydroxy ketones are an

00:05:45.828 --> 00:05:46.628
important exception.

00:05:47.448 --> 00:05:48.748
Consider acetone.

00:05:49.588 --> 00:05:54.008
Hydride reduction gives isopropyl alcohol with three carbons and oxygen still

00:05:54.008 --> 00:05:54.368
present.

00:05:55.708 --> 00:06:00.346
Complete carbonyl deoxygenation instead gives propane with three carbons and

00:06:00.346 --> 00:06:00.468
no

00:06:00.468 --> 00:06:01.968
oxygen in the organic product.

00:06:03.048 --> 00:06:07.508
Tollens oxidation is another change: acetaldehyde gives an acetate product

00:06:07.508 --> 00:06:09.068
under the basic test conditions.

00:06:10.188 --> 00:06:14.368
Acid work up changes the protonation state, so do not confuse a carboxylate

00:06:14.368 --> 00:06:15.968
salt with an uncharged acid.

00:06:16.296 --> 00:06:20.156
Hydrogen on a carbon adjacent to a carbonyl is an alpha hydrogen.

00:06:21.256 --> 00:06:25.396
Removing it with a suitable base produces an enolate stabilized by resonance.

00:06:26.316 --> 00:06:30.408
In an aldol addition, this carbon nucleophile attacks another carbonyl

00:06:30.408 --> 00:06:30.876
molecule

00:06:30.876 --> 00:06:33.736
to form a beta hydroxy carbonyl product.

00:06:34.836 --> 00:06:37.976
Dehydration can then give an alpha,beta unsaturated product.

00:06:39.196 --> 00:06:44.256
acetaldehyde gives three hydroxybutanal in its simple self aldol addition, and

00:06:44.256 --> 00:06:47.256
heating can produce but two enal.

00:06:47.256 --> 00:06:51.696
Benzaldehyde has no alpha hydrogen, so it cannot undergo the same self aldol

00:06:51.696 --> 00:06:51.956
route.

00:06:52.796 --> 00:06:56.476
With concentrated hydroxide it can undergo Cannizzaro disproportionation,

00:06:57.336 --> 00:06:59.036
giving benzoate and benzyl alcohol.

00:07:00.076 --> 00:07:03.650
Lack of alpha hydrogen distinguishes the elementary pathways; crossed

00:07:03.650 --> 00:07:04.116
reactions

00:07:04.116 --> 00:07:05.756
require more careful analysis.

00:07:06.976 --> 00:07:08.496
Carbon counting is a strong check.

00:07:09.256 --> 00:07:13.116
Two molecules of the two carbon aldehyde combine to give a four carbon aldol

00:07:13.116 --> 00:07:13.556
product.

00:07:14.396 --> 00:07:18.916
The initial addition retains all the oxygen and hydrogen atoms overall; later

00:07:18.916 --> 00:07:22.016
dehydration removes water and introduces a double bond.

00:07:22.936 --> 00:07:27.416
In Cannizzaro chemistry, one aldehyde molecule is oxidized while another is

00:07:27.416 --> 00:07:27.756
reduced.

00:07:28.296 --> 00:07:31.796
It is disproportionation, not a carbon chain doubling reaction.

00:07:32.016 --> 00:07:36.976
Methyl ketones contain a methyl carbonyl group and can give yellow iodoform

00:07:36.976 --> 00:07:38.976
with iodine and base.

00:07:39.776 --> 00:07:44.116
Alcohols oxidizable to that pattern can also respond, as can ethanol through

00:07:44.116 --> 00:07:45.216
acetaldehyde.

00:07:45.716 --> 00:07:49.576
isopropyl alcohol is positive; one propanol normally is not.

00:07:50.296 --> 00:07:54.516
Therefore a positive result does not prove the original sample was already a

00:07:54.516 --> 00:07:54.556
ketone.

00:07:55.456 --> 00:07:57.316
Do not combine test rules blindly.

00:07:58.236 --> 00:08:02.456
acetaldehyde is both an aldehyde and an iodoform positive compound;

00:08:03.236 --> 00:08:07.356
benzaldehyde can give Tollens' response but lacks the methyl carbonyl pattern.

00:08:08.536 --> 00:08:12.456
Carbonyl derivative formation helps establish a carbonyl group but does not

00:08:12.456 --> 00:08:15.016
alone distinguish every aldehyde from every ketone.

00:08:15.996 --> 00:08:19.893
Acetone contains a methyl group directly attached to its carbonyl carbon and

00:08:19.893 --> 00:08:19.996
is

00:08:19.996 --> 00:08:21.436
a standard positive example.

00:08:22.456 --> 00:08:26.207
Diethyl ketone lacks that methyl ketone arrangement and is ordinarily

00:08:26.207 --> 00:08:26.696
negative.

00:08:27.776 --> 00:08:31.236
Compare the carbonyl carbon's immediate neighbours rather than looking for a

00:08:31.236 --> 00:08:32.776
methyl group anywhere in the molecule.

00:08:33.796 --> 00:08:38.116
Use more than one observation for identification, since one positive response

00:08:38.116 --> 00:08:40.736
can correspond to several functional groups or precursors.

00:08:41.472 --> 00:08:46.032
Carboxylic acids can release carbon dioxide from bicarbonate, helping

00:08:46.032 --> 00:08:48.712
distinguish them from ordinary phenols and alcohols.

00:08:49.232 --> 00:08:52.292
Their conjugate bases are resonance stabilized carboxylates.

00:08:52.992 --> 00:08:56.814
An electron withdrawing substituent generally stabilizes nearby negative

00:08:56.814 --> 00:08:57.132
charge

00:08:57.132 --> 00:09:01.100
and increases acidity, with an inductive effect that weakens as the

00:09:01.100 --> 00:09:01.752
substituent

00:09:01.752 --> 00:09:02.752
becomes more distant.

00:09:03.392 --> 00:09:07.872
For comparable aqueous conditions, chloroacetic acid is stronger than acetic

00:09:07.872 --> 00:09:10.732
acid because chlorine withdraws electron density.

00:09:11.852 --> 00:09:16.217
Acid catalysed esterification with an alcohol is reversible, so composition

00:09:16.217 --> 00:09:16.392
and

00:09:16.392 --> 00:09:17.912
water removal affect yield.

00:09:18.772 --> 00:09:22.412
Exam answers should track functional groups, reagent conditions and carbon

00:09:22.412 --> 00:09:25.192
count before applying an isolated reaction name.

00:09:26.132 --> 00:09:29.452
Chlorine's inductive effect is strongest when it lies near the carboxyl group

00:09:29.452 --> 00:09:32.012
and diminishes with separation along the chain.

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:36.532
Replacing a chlorine substituent by a different group requires a fresh

00:09:36.532 --> 00:09:39.592
comparison rather than a universal ranking by atom count.

00:09:40.512 --> 00:09:44.912
In esterification, an acid and alcohol supply the two carbon portions of the

00:09:44.912 --> 00:09:45.232
ester.

00:09:45.872 --> 00:09:50.152
Acetic acid plus ethanol gives a four carbon ester and water under suitable

00:09:50.152 --> 00:09:51.212
equilibrium conditions.

00:09:51.528 --> 00:09:54.308
An ether links two carbon groups through oxygen.

00:09:55.208 --> 00:09:59.448
In Williamson synthesis, an alkoxide attacks a suitable alkyl halide by

00:09:59.448 --> 00:10:00.548
backside substitution.

00:10:01.508 --> 00:10:05.708
A primary electrophile is usually preferred because increasing crowding can

00:10:05.708 --> 00:10:08.488
favour elimination rather than the desired substitution.

00:10:09.468 --> 00:10:14.488
To prepare ethyl tert butyl ether conceptually, using tert butoxide with an

00:10:14.488 --> 00:10:18.388
appropriate ethyl halide is more suitable than asking ethoxide to displace a

00:10:18.388 --> 00:10:20.108
leaving group at a tertiary carbon.

00:10:20.708 --> 00:10:25.008
The same product formula does not make both proposed routes equally effective.

00:10:26.148 --> 00:10:29.600
Strong hydrogen halides can cleave appropriate ethers after oxygen

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:30.228
protonation.

00:10:31.108 --> 00:10:34.868
With an aryl methyl ether, cleavage at the methyl side can give methyl halide

00:10:34.868 --> 00:10:39.468
and phenol; ordinary backside attack does not simply displace oxygen at an

00:10:39.468 --> 00:10:40.088
aromatic carbon.

00:10:40.848 --> 00:10:44.788
For anisole with suitable hydrogen iodide conditions, the conceptual products

00:10:44.788 --> 00:10:46.508
are phenol and methyl iodide.

00:10:47.548 --> 00:10:51.028
Cleavage extent and products depend on substrate, reagent amount and

00:10:51.028 --> 00:10:51.428
conditions.

00:10:52.488 --> 00:10:56.168
Distinguish ether cleavage from aromatic ring substitution, and check which

00:10:56.168 --> 00:10:59.388
carbon oxygen bond actually breaks before drawing the products.

00:10:59.928 --> 00:11:05.068
A Grignard reagent contributes a carbon group to a suitable carbonyl compound.

00:11:05.948 --> 00:11:09.528
After the addition stage, protonation of the oxygen gives an alcohol.

00:11:10.308 --> 00:11:14.328
Keep the carbonyl carbon in the product and attach the incoming group to it.

00:11:15.048 --> 00:11:19.008
Formaldehyde gives a primary alcohol, other simple aldehydes give secondary

00:11:19.008 --> 00:11:23.388
alcohols, and ketones give tertiary alcohols under suitable conditions.

00:11:23.888 --> 00:11:28.828
For methylmagnesium bromide and formaldehyde, one incoming carbon joins one

00:11:28.828 --> 00:11:33.308
carbonyl carbon, giving a two carbon primary alcohol after work up.

00:11:33.308 --> 00:11:37.428
With acetaldehyde, the same methyl reagent gives a three carbon secondary

00:11:37.428 --> 00:11:39.348
alcohol, isopropyl alcohol.

00:11:40.088 --> 00:11:44.608
With acetone, it gives a four carbon tertiary alcohol, tert butyl alcohol.

00:11:45.168 --> 00:11:49.228
These products demonstrate that alcohol classification depends on the final

00:11:49.228 --> 00:11:50.428
carbon carrying oxygen.

00:11:50.968 --> 00:11:55.348
A molecule containing an acidic proton can quench the reagent before addition,

00:11:55.928 --> 00:12:00.148
so a carboxylic acid cannot be treated like an uncharged ketone in this

00:12:00.148 --> 00:12:00.488
prediction.

00:12:01.608 --> 00:12:05.668
Identify incompatible groups and the order of addition before using a carbon

00:12:05.668 --> 00:12:06.528
counting shortcut.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:10.700
A reducing agent must be chosen for the actual functional group.

00:12:11.600 --> 00:12:16.380
In the ordinary introductory comparison, sodium borohydride reduces aldehydes

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:18.500
and ketones to alcohols.

00:12:19.200 --> 00:12:24.320
Formaldehyde gives methanol; acetaldehyde gives ethanol; acetone gives the

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.440
secondary alcohol isopropyl alcohol.

00:12:27.400 --> 00:12:31.520
The carbonyl carbon remains in the product, so no carbon is added by this

00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:31.800
reduction.

00:12:32.520 --> 00:12:37.600
A suitable lithium aluminium hydride reduction followed by workup can also

00:12:37.600 --> 00:12:40.360
convert a carboxylic acid into a primary alcohol.

00:12:41.600 --> 00:12:45.280
Ethanoic acid therefore gives ethanol while retaining its two carbons.

00:12:46.520 --> 00:12:50.671
Do not assign this ordinary acid reduction to sodium borohydride merely

00:12:50.671 --> 00:12:51.080
because

00:12:51.080 --> 00:12:52.780
both reagents contain hydrogen.

00:12:53.820 --> 00:12:57.260
Reagent selectivity and the workup are part of the stated transformation.

00:12:57.940 --> 00:13:02.200
Reduction of a ketone creates a secondary alcohol, whereas reduction of an

00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:05.140
aldehyde other than formaldehyde creates a primary alcohol.

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:09.831
A carbonyl attached to different substituents can also create a new

00:13:09.831 --> 00:13:10.420
stereogenic

00:13:10.420 --> 00:13:15.220
centre after reduction, so a flat formula alone may not describe all

00:13:15.220 --> 00:13:16.300
stereochemical products.

00:13:17.580 --> 00:13:21.740
Start with functional class, check reagent scope, then verify the carbon count

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:23.060
and final oxygen bonding.

00:13:23.400 --> 00:13:27.660
Chemical tests are useful when their scope and exceptions are understood.

00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:33.920
In common introductory examples, aldehydes can reduce Tollens reagent while

00:13:33.920 --> 00:13:35.380
ordinary ketones do not.

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:40.860
Some other reducing structures, including suitable alpha hydroxy ketones, can

00:13:40.860 --> 00:13:45.680
also respond, so a positive result is not a unique molecular identification.

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:51.820
Two four dinitrophenylhydrazine tests for many aldehyde and ketone carbonyl

00:13:51.820 --> 00:13:56.580
groups through derivative formation; it does not by itself distinguish the two

00:13:56.580 --> 00:13:56.980
classes.

00:13:57.720 --> 00:14:02.160
The iodoform test instead points to a methyl ketone or a structure that can

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:04.880
form the required intermediate under the test conditions.

00:14:05.920 --> 00:14:10.720
Ethanol and isopropyl alcohol are familiar positive alcohol examples, whereas

00:14:10.720 --> 00:14:14.000
ordinary propyl alcohol is negative in the standard comparison.

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:19.260
A ferric chloride colour response can support a phenolic group but is not a

00:14:19.260 --> 00:14:21.620
universal proof of one particular phenol.

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:25.560
Combine observations with the molecular formula and other evidence.

00:14:26.420 --> 00:14:29.840
Never infer a complete structure from one colour or precipitate alone.

00:14:30.760 --> 00:14:34.460
These are principles for interpreting supplied observations, rather than

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:36.580
instructions for carrying out laboratory tests.

00:14:37.248 --> 00:14:42.168
Two named phenol transformations add a carbon but give different functional

00:14:42.168 --> 00:14:42.528
groups.

00:14:43.328 --> 00:14:47.548
In the standard Reimer Tiemann reaction, phenol with chloroform and aqueous

00:14:47.548 --> 00:14:52.028
base followed by the stated workup gives mainly an ortho formyl product.

00:14:52.548 --> 00:14:57.228
The familiar product is salicylaldehyde, also called two hydroxybenzaldehyde.

00:14:57.708 --> 00:15:00.748
Its new group is an aldehyde, not a carboxylic acid.

00:15:01.268 --> 00:15:05.388
In the Kolbe Schmitt transformation, a phenoxide reacts with carbon dioxide

00:15:05.388 --> 00:15:10.128
under appropriate conditions and acid workup gives salicylic acid, or two

00:15:10.128 --> 00:15:13.028
hydroxybenzoic acid, as the familiar main product.

00:15:13.848 --> 00:15:16.208
Here the new carbon belongs to a carboxyl group.

00:15:16.928 --> 00:15:21.118
Both products have seven carbons compared with phenol's six, but they differ

00:15:21.118 --> 00:15:21.228
in

00:15:21.228 --> 00:15:23.348
oxidation level and functional class.

00:15:23.888 --> 00:15:28.108
The directing pattern is a useful introductory outcome, not a claim that no

00:15:28.108 --> 00:15:29.948
other positional product can ever form.

00:15:30.648 --> 00:15:33.928
Keep the reagent source of the added carbon visible in a reaction map.

00:15:33.928 --> 00:15:37.808
Chloroform and carbon dioxide are not interchangeable labels.

00:15:38.808 --> 00:15:42.928
Identify the new group first, then name the product and count the ring plus

00:15:42.928 --> 00:15:43.888
substituent carbons.

00:15:45.000 --> 00:15:49.160
Aldol and Cannizzaro questions require inspecting the carbon next to the

00:15:49.160 --> 00:15:49.560
carbonyl.

00:15:50.280 --> 00:15:54.420
Acetaldehyde has alpha hydrogens and can form an enolate under suitable base

00:15:54.420 --> 00:15:54.880
conditions.

00:15:55.700 --> 00:15:59.898
Two molecules first give a four carbon hydroxy aldehyde with formula C H

00:15:59.898 --> 00:16:00.248
three,

00:16:00.248 --> 00:16:04.400
C H with O H, C H two, C H O.

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:07.780
Subsequent dehydration gives the unsaturated aldehyde commonly called

00:16:07.780 --> 00:16:10.380
crotonaldehyde, with four carbons still present.

00:16:11.140 --> 00:16:12.800
Water loss does not remove a carbon.

00:16:13.640 --> 00:16:17.680
Benzaldehyde lacks an alpha hydrogen at its carbonyl substituent, so it does

00:16:17.680 --> 00:16:20.060
not undergo that ordinary self aldol pathway.

00:16:20.940 --> 00:16:25.000
Under suitable concentrated base conditions, two benzaldehyde molecules can

00:16:25.000 --> 00:16:27.740
instead disproportionate through the Cannizzaro reaction.

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:31.520
One becomes benzyl alcohol and the other a benzoate ion.

00:16:32.140 --> 00:16:36.160
Each organic product retains seven carbons; the two molecules have not joined

00:16:36.160 --> 00:16:37.880
into one fourteen carbon product.

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:41.160
Acid workup converts benzoate to benzoic acid.

00:16:42.120 --> 00:16:45.060
State whether the question asks for the initial addition product, the

00:16:45.060 --> 00:16:47.900
dehydrated product or the material after workup.

00:16:48.300 --> 00:16:52.020
These choices change the formula and functional groups even when the carbon

00:16:52.020 --> 00:16:53.300
accounting remains straightforward.

00:16:53.664 --> 00:16:58.144
Esterification is reversible, so a catalyst does not guarantee complete

00:16:58.144 --> 00:16:58.604
conversion.

00:16:59.644 --> 00:17:04.004
Consider an illustrative homogeneous ideal equilibrium model for one mole of

00:17:04.004 --> 00:17:08.364
ethanoic acid and one mole of ethanol, initially without ester or water.

00:17:08.984 --> 00:17:13.884
The balanced reaction forms one mole each of ethyl ethanoate and water for

00:17:13.884 --> 00:17:15.284
every mole of acid consumed.

00:17:16.304 --> 00:17:17.964
Let the extent in moles be x.

00:17:18.484 --> 00:17:22.848
Remaining acid and alcohol are each one minus x, while ester and water are

00:17:22.848 --> 00:17:23.084
each

00:17:23.084 --> 00:17:23.524
x.

00:17:24.284 --> 00:17:28.724
For a supplied equilibrium constant of four in this ideal ratio model, the

00:17:28.724 --> 00:17:33.284
square of x divided by the square of the quantity one minus x equals four.

00:17:34.164 --> 00:17:38.764
Take the positive square root to obtain x divided by one minus x equals two.

00:17:39.644 --> 00:17:43.924
Solving gives x equal to two thirds, so about sixty six point seven percent of

00:17:43.924 --> 00:17:45.504
each starting reactant is converted.

00:17:46.204 --> 00:17:50.004
Check that every amount stays nonnegative and the quotient returns four.

00:17:50.984 --> 00:17:53.704
Actual mixtures need their appropriate activities and conditions.

00:17:54.424 --> 00:17:57.978
A catalyst speeds approach to equilibrium at fixed temperature without

00:17:57.978 --> 00:17:58.384
changing

00:17:58.384 --> 00:17:59.064
its constant.

00:17:59.984 --> 00:18:02.464
Removing a product can alter the equilibrium composition.

00:18:02.712 --> 00:18:07.972
An aldol question has two roles: an enolate donor and a carbonyl acceptor.

00:18:08.372 --> 00:18:13.152
If acetaldehyde and propionaldehyde are mixed under an idealized ordinary base

00:18:13.152 --> 00:18:17.904
catalyzed aldol model, both have alpha hydrogens and can supply enolate

00:18:17.904 --> 00:18:18.372
donors.

00:18:19.312 --> 00:18:21.432
Each donor can attack either aldehyde.

00:18:22.092 --> 00:18:26.492
The elementary first addition list therefore has two self aldol connectivities

00:18:26.492 --> 00:18:29.832
and two crossed connectivities, four in total.

00:18:30.692 --> 00:18:35.592
Acetaldehyde acting as donor and propionaldehyde as acceptor is not the same

00:18:35.592 --> 00:18:39.972
connectivity as propionaldehyde donating to acetaldehyde, because the carbonyl

00:18:39.972 --> 00:18:42.472
group retained in the product belongs to the donor.

00:18:43.552 --> 00:18:47.332
Stereoisomers and later dehydration products can enlarge a more detailed

00:18:47.332 --> 00:18:48.552
product list.

00:18:48.992 --> 00:18:53.052
Thus four is a restricted connectivity count, not a complete description of

00:18:53.052 --> 00:18:55.912
every species present after unrestricted reaction.

00:18:56.792 --> 00:19:01.038
Benzaldehyde has no alpha hydrogen at its aldehyde side chain and cannot

00:19:01.038 --> 00:19:01.392
supply

00:19:01.392 --> 00:19:03.272
the ordinary enolate in this comparison.

00:19:04.412 --> 00:19:08.092
Pairing it with a suitable enolizable carbonyl can reduce the donor choices,

00:19:08.692 --> 00:19:11.672
but selectivity still depends on the specified conditions.

00:19:12.752 --> 00:19:16.552
Identify which partner donates, which carbonyl is attacked and whether the

00:19:16.552 --> 00:19:20.752
question asks for addition or condensation before drawing a single preferred

00:19:20.752 --> 00:19:21.192
product.

00:19:21.768 --> 00:19:26.968
Ester hydrolysis should distinguish an acid catalyzed equilibrium from a base

00:19:26.968 --> 00:19:28.128
promoted net conversion.

00:19:29.148 --> 00:19:34.228
For ethyl ethanoate, acid catalyzed hydrolysis connects the ester and water

00:19:34.228 --> 00:19:36.368
with ethanoic acid and ethanol.

00:19:37.268 --> 00:19:41.428
The catalyst changes the route and rate, but not the equilibrium constant at a

00:19:41.428 --> 00:19:41.828
fixed temperature.

00:19:42.488 --> 00:19:47.448
In the basic net equation, hydroxide is consumed and the acid derived product

00:19:47.448 --> 00:19:50.808
remains mainly carboxylate under the stated basic conditions.

00:19:51.708 --> 00:19:56.068
One mole of this simple ester consumes one mole hydroxide to give one mole

00:19:56.068 --> 00:19:57.808
ethanoate and one mole ethanol.

00:19:59.208 --> 00:20:03.008
Suppose an illustrative mixture contains zero point zero two zero zero mole

00:20:03.008 --> 00:20:08.208
ester and zero point zero one five zero mole hydroxide, and assume this net

00:20:08.208 --> 00:20:12.088
reaction reaches the stated stoichiometric endpoint with no competing

00:20:12.088 --> 00:20:12.688
processes.

00:20:13.688 --> 00:20:17.368
Hydroxide limits conversion to zero point zero one five zero mole.

00:20:18.488 --> 00:20:23.362
Ester remaining is zero point zero zero five zero zero mole, and ethanol

00:20:23.362 --> 00:20:23.768
formed

00:20:23.768 --> 00:20:26.228
is zero point zero one five zero mole.

00:20:27.188 --> 00:20:30.828
Do not describe hydroxide as a recovered acid catalyst in that balance.

00:20:31.708 --> 00:20:35.348
The carboxylate is not the isolated neutral acid unless a separate

00:20:35.348 --> 00:20:36.588
acidification is specified.

00:20:36.588 --> 00:20:40.948
This calculation concerns chemical accounting rather than a practical

00:20:40.948 --> 00:20:41.468
preparation.

00:20:42.024 --> 00:20:45.704
A carbonyl addition product can introduce both a new carbon and a

00:20:45.704 --> 00:20:46.724
stereochemical question.

00:20:47.584 --> 00:20:51.964
In cyanohydrin formation, the cyanide carbon forms a bond to the carbonyl

00:20:51.964 --> 00:20:54.664
carbon, followed by proton transfer to oxygen.

00:20:55.424 --> 00:21:00.064
For acetaldehyde, the product has a carbon bearing hydroxyl, hydrogen, methyl

00:21:00.284 --> 00:21:01.144
and cyano groups.

00:21:01.824 --> 00:21:06.044
These four groups differ, so the former planar carbonyl carbon becomes a

00:21:06.044 --> 00:21:07.304
stereogenic centre.

00:21:07.304 --> 00:21:11.984
In an achiral environment with no chiral influence, attack at the two

00:21:11.984 --> 00:21:15.558
enantiotopic faces gives the two enantiomers in equal amounts in the

00:21:15.558 --> 00:21:16.084
elementary

00:21:16.084 --> 00:21:16.364
model.

00:21:17.064 --> 00:21:20.524
The mixture is racemic, rather than a single assigned configuration.

00:21:21.464 --> 00:21:25.684
Acetaldehyde contributes two carbons and the cyano group one, giving three

00:21:25.684 --> 00:21:26.404
carbons overall.

00:21:26.924 --> 00:21:31.270
The corresponding propanone cyanohydrin contains two identical methyl groups

00:21:31.270 --> 00:21:31.384
at

00:21:31.384 --> 00:21:35.767
the hydroxyl bearing carbon, so that carbon is not stereogenic despite the

00:21:35.767 --> 00:21:36.004
same

00:21:36.004 --> 00:21:36.964
general addition pattern.

00:21:37.604 --> 00:21:40.384
A carbon count alone therefore cannot establish chirality.

00:21:41.044 --> 00:21:44.884
The process is reversible and its extent depends on substrate and conditions;

00:21:45.504 --> 00:21:48.384
the connectivity exercise does not assert complete conversion.

00:21:49.244 --> 00:21:53.104
Read the cyano carbon explicitly instead of confusing nucleophilic addition

00:21:53.104 --> 00:21:55.204
with replacement of an existing carbon atom.

00:21:56.540 --> 00:22:00.900
Acid strength and the amount of acid in a solution answer different questions.

00:22:01.800 --> 00:22:06.020
Compare two supplied monoprotic carboxylic acids in the same solvent at the

00:22:06.020 --> 00:22:09.780
same temperature, with p K a values three and five.

00:22:10.520 --> 00:22:14.440
The lower p K a corresponds to the larger acid dissociation constant.

00:22:15.180 --> 00:22:20.100
Their constant ratio is ten squared, or one hundred, so the first acid is

00:22:20.100 --> 00:22:21.480
stronger on that defined scale.

00:22:22.060 --> 00:22:26.579
This does not mean every equal concentration solution has one hundred times

00:22:26.579 --> 00:22:26.760
the

00:22:26.760 --> 00:22:27.860
hydrogen ion concentration.

00:22:28.680 --> 00:22:33.040
Dissociation must also satisfy mass balance and any approximations used.

00:22:33.780 --> 00:22:38.500
In an ideal weak acid range, where both acids remain only slightly ionized and

00:22:38.500 --> 00:22:42.483
water ionization is negligible, hydrogen ion concentration scales

00:22:42.483 --> 00:22:43.280
approximately

00:22:43.280 --> 00:22:46.820
with the square root of K a times analytical concentration.

00:22:47.400 --> 00:22:52.160
At equal concentration, a hundredfold K a ratio would then give roughly a

00:22:52.160 --> 00:22:53.820
tenfold hydrogen ion ratio.

00:22:54.720 --> 00:22:58.440
Check the small ionization assumption for each acid before applying that

00:22:58.440 --> 00:22:58.680
estimate.

00:22:59.600 --> 00:23:03.800
Electron withdrawing substituents can stabilize a carboxylate and increase

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:07.060
acidity, while their distance and structural position matter.

00:23:07.880 --> 00:23:11.460
Supplied same solvent data support the numerical ranking without pretending

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:15.000
that a substituent name alone determines every measured p H.

00:23:15.000 --> 00:23:19.480
Aldehydes and ketones can form nitrogen containing derivatives by condensation

00:23:19.480 --> 00:23:21.540
with suitable nitrogen nucleophiles.

00:23:22.380 --> 00:23:26.720
Hydroxylamine gives an oxime, replacing the carbonyl double bond with a carbon

00:23:26.720 --> 00:23:30.700
nitrogen double bond while retaining an oxygen hydrogen group on nitrogen.

00:23:31.560 --> 00:23:36.020
Hydrazine gives a hydrazone with a carbon nitrogen double bond and an N H two

00:23:36.020 --> 00:23:37.600
group attached to that nitrogen.

00:23:37.600 --> 00:23:42.440
In each simple net equation, one water molecule is eliminated per carbonyl

00:23:42.440 --> 00:23:43.040
group converted.

00:23:43.560 --> 00:23:48.540
For acetone plus hydroxylamine, the product oxime has three carbons, seven

00:23:48.540 --> 00:23:51.020
hydrogens, one nitrogen and one oxygen.

00:23:52.020 --> 00:23:56.100
Acetone plus hydrazine instead gives the corresponding hydrazone with three

00:23:56.100 --> 00:23:58.440
carbons, eight hydrogens and two nitrogens.

00:23:59.240 --> 00:24:01.720
No carbon has been added by either nitrogen reagent.

00:24:02.560 --> 00:24:06.500
Acetone's two identical methyl groups also mean its simple oxime does not give

00:24:06.500 --> 00:24:09.140
a distinct E and Z pair at the carbon nitrogen bond.

00:24:09.600 --> 00:24:13.902
An unsymmetrical carbonyl derivative may require a geometrical isomerism

00:24:13.902 --> 00:24:14.260
check.

00:24:15.300 --> 00:24:19.020
Formation and hydrolysis depend on conditions; these net equations do not mean

00:24:19.020 --> 00:24:20.640
every mixture converts completely.

00:24:21.600 --> 00:24:25.060
Count the reagent atoms and eliminated water before assigning a derivative

00:24:25.060 --> 00:24:25.400
formula.

00:24:25.656 --> 00:24:29.816
Phenol's hydroxyl group strongly activates its ring toward electrophilic

00:24:29.816 --> 00:24:32.736
substitution and favours ortho and para positions.

00:24:33.536 --> 00:24:38.033
In the standard introductory bromine water comparison, phenol can form two

00:24:38.033 --> 00:24:38.276
four

00:24:38.276 --> 00:24:41.996
six tribromophenol, with three ring hydrogens replaced by bromine.

00:24:42.556 --> 00:24:46.516
The net equation uses three bromine molecules for each phenol and produces

00:24:46.516 --> 00:24:48.056
three hydrogen bromide units.

00:24:48.696 --> 00:24:51.976
The oxygen hydrogen bond is retained in this ring substitution ledger.

00:24:52.856 --> 00:24:57.076
With phenol molar mass ninety four and bromine atom mass eighty, the

00:24:57.076 --> 00:25:00.636
tribrominated product has molar mass three hundred thirty one grams per mole,

00:25:01.236 --> 00:25:04.336
because three hydrogens of mass one are replaced by three bromines.

00:25:05.176 --> 00:25:09.156
Thus zero point zero one zero zero mole product has theoretical mass three

00:25:09.156 --> 00:25:11.816
point three one grams using those supplied values.

00:25:13.076 --> 00:25:16.717
Under different controlled low polarity conditions, monobromination can

00:25:16.717 --> 00:25:17.076
instead

00:25:17.076 --> 00:25:20.036
be favoured, commonly giving ortho and para products.

00:25:20.546 --> 00:25:24.576
Do not transfer the three bromine stoichiometry into every phenol bromination

00:25:24.576 --> 00:25:27.536
question without reading the stated medium and extent.

00:25:28.236 --> 00:25:31.656
A precipitate observation supports a reaction pattern, not a unique

00:25:31.656 --> 00:25:34.176
identification of every unknown phenolic sample.

00:25:34.416 --> 00:25:38.910
Two carboxylate transformations can lose the carboxyl carbon yet give

00:25:38.910 --> 00:25:39.496
different

00:25:39.496 --> 00:25:40.596
hydrocarbon sizes.

00:25:41.456 --> 00:25:45.856
In the standard soda lime decarboxylation model, sodium propanoate gives

00:25:45.856 --> 00:25:50.753
ethane: the three carbon salt loses its carboxyl carbon into carbonate,

00:25:50.753 --> 00:25:51.236
leaving

00:25:51.236 --> 00:25:52.556
a two carbon hydrocarbon.

00:25:53.196 --> 00:25:58.300
A balanced net equation combines sodium propanoate and sodium hydroxide to

00:25:58.300 --> 00:25:58.576
form

00:25:58.576 --> 00:26:00.056
ethane and sodium carbonate.

00:26:00.856 --> 00:26:06.296
In the ideal Kolbe electrolysis comparison, two propanoate ions each lose one

00:26:06.296 --> 00:26:09.136
electron and one carbon dioxide unit at the anode.

00:26:09.736 --> 00:26:13.982
The resulting two ethyl radicals couple to form butane, a four carbon

00:26:13.982 --> 00:26:14.536
molecule.

00:26:15.396 --> 00:26:20.076
The anodic net equation is two propanoate ions forming butane, two carbon

00:26:20.076 --> 00:26:21.936
dioxide molecules and two electrons.

00:26:22.516 --> 00:26:26.916
One mole of coupled butane therefore corresponds to two moles of electrons in

00:26:26.916 --> 00:26:28.176
that ideal anodic model.

00:26:29.036 --> 00:26:33.536
This is a carbon and charge ledger, not a statement that electrolysis always

00:26:33.536 --> 00:26:35.476
gives a single product at full efficiency.

00:26:36.796 --> 00:26:40.156
Competing reactions and substrate structure can affect the actual product

00:26:40.156 --> 00:26:40.556
mixture.

00:26:41.696 --> 00:26:45.116
Distinguish these transformations from the Kolbe Schmitt carboxylation of

00:26:45.116 --> 00:26:49.556
phenoxide, which has a similar name but adds a carboxyl group to an aromatic

00:26:49.556 --> 00:26:49.996
system.

00:26:50.184 --> 00:26:54.884
An ester can react with ammonia under suitable conditions to give an amide and

00:26:54.884 --> 00:26:55.424
an alcohol.

00:26:56.164 --> 00:27:00.604
In the simple ethyl ethanoate example, the acyl fragment contains two carbons

00:27:00.604 --> 00:27:03.224
and the ethoxy fragment contains two more.

00:27:04.164 --> 00:27:08.784
Aminolysis produces ethanamide from the two carbon acyl fragment and ethanol

00:27:08.784 --> 00:27:10.744
from the two carbon ethoxy fragment.

00:27:11.284 --> 00:27:14.844
The total carbon count remains four across the two organic products.

00:27:15.624 --> 00:27:20.340
The net equation consumes one ammonia per ester and does not eliminate water

00:27:20.340 --> 00:27:20.464
in

00:27:20.464 --> 00:27:21.744
this ester to amide accounting.

00:27:22.404 --> 00:27:26.364
That differs from writing a direct carboxylic acid plus ammonia condensation

00:27:26.364 --> 00:27:29.564
without considering initial salt formation or conditions.

00:27:30.184 --> 00:27:34.884
In the usual nucleophilic acyl substitution description, ammonia attacks the

00:27:34.884 --> 00:27:39.904
carbonyl carbon, a tetrahedral intermediate forms, proton transfers occur and

00:27:39.904 --> 00:27:41.704
the alcohol derived group departs.

00:27:41.964 --> 00:27:45.144
This is not ordinary backside displacement at the ethyl carbon.

00:27:46.064 --> 00:27:50.444
A neutral amide product contains a carbonyl bonded to nitrogen, while the

00:27:50.444 --> 00:27:54.644
alcohol retains the ester alkoxy oxygen in this simple unlabelled structural

00:27:54.644 --> 00:27:54.944
ledger.

00:27:56.184 --> 00:28:00.084
Reaction rate, competing hydrolysis and conversion depend on the actual ester

00:28:00.084 --> 00:28:03.704
and conditions; the net equation alone supplies stoichiometry.
